From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 02:45:48 +0200
Subject: media: sun6i-isp: Support both output channels

The ISP writes out through two channels, main and self, each with its own
size, format and buffer addresses in its own register block. Add the
second video device, and with it the two things the hardware needs before
two channels can work at all.

The self channel does not honour its own registers unless the main channel
is enabled alongside it. On its own it ignores them and lays down a full
sized 4:2:2 frame - 1843200 bytes at 720p whatever was asked for, at every
size and format tried - overrunning any buffer smaller than that. The
main channel through the same code is right every time, and the two
together are right as well; the vendor's bsp_isp_set_fmt() has the same
shape, programming the self channel's format only when a second channel
is in play. So the blocks are handed out rather than owned: the main block
goes to whichever channel is streaming, and the self block is only used
when both are.

That assignment cannot change while the pipeline runs. The channel
registers are only honoured from the frontend start that latched them, so
a channel joining or leaving is applied by taking the pipeline down and
bringing it back up around the new set - sun6i_isp_pipeline_restart(). The
buffers in flight go back to the head of their own queues first, so the
frame the hardware was part way through is written again rather than
handed over torn. The channels that keep streaming lose about three frames
to it, which is the price of two outputs behind one frontend.

Every configuration pass writes both blocks, and one with no channel
behind it is written disabled rather than left describing an earlier
stream with only its enable bit standing between that and the hardware.

The capture links become mutable, so a pipeline can leave out the channel
it has no use for instead of having its format validated anyway.

The stream lock is new: videobuf2 holds a queue lock per channel, which
does not serialise the two channels against each other, and the restart
must not interleave with itself.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
